  <published>2026-11-02T05:00:17+00:00</published>
  <updated>2026-11-05T04:13:12+00:00</updated>
  <media:group>
   <media:title>Office Hours with Nathalie Pinkerton: Targeting Oral Cancer Pain with Nanomedicine</media:title>
   <media:content url="https://www.youtube.com/v/RyPUu0U4uvg?version=3" type="application/x-shockwave-flash" width="640" height="390"/>
   <media:thumbnail url="https://i3.ytimg.com/vi/RyPUu0U4uvg/hqdefault.jpg" width="480" height="360"/>
   <media:description>How can nanoparticles deliver longer-lasting pain relief? NYU Tandon’s Nathalie May Pinkerton designs tiny drug carriers that enter specific cells, release therapies slowly, and inhibit signaling pathways involved in oral cancer pain. In collaboration with NYU Dentistry researchers, her team has shown that nanoparticle delivery reduces sensitivity to heat and pressure, with effects lasting longer than the drug alone. Step inside her lab to learn how materials chemistry, light-responsive nanoparticles, and scalable manufacturing could help advance new treatments for chronic pain.

The NYU Tandon School of Engineering is home to a vibrant community of world-class researchers, ambitious students, leading industry partners, and groundbreaking founders uniting to solve humanity’s greatest challenges.
engineering.nyu.edu/impact</media:description>
